Fix: the HIT_DIVERGED RECONCILE fallback (v1/core/sched/scheduler.py, see
patches/hit-diverged-reconcile-log-dedup.patch for the log-spam half of
this same investigation) redid its entire lookup/rescue/reconcile dance
from scratch on every single scheduler tick for a request stuck waiting
on GPU memory, not just re-logging identically -- the log-spam patch
only silenced the symptom. This patch addresses the underlying wasted
work the user asked to have investigated after the first fix landed.

Root cause: `if request.num_computed_tokens == 0:` gates the entire
local-lookup + connector round-trip + boundary-rescue + reconcile block,
and `num_computed_tokens` is only ever set once `allocate_slots()`
actually succeeds (`request.num_computed_tokens = num_computed_tokens`,
well below this block). When `allocate_slots()` returns None (no free KV
blocks -- confirmed from the user's own paste: GPU KV cache usage
81-99.6%, 3 running + up to 1 waiting/deferred request), the scheduling
loop `break`s for that step without ever setting num_computed_tokens, so
the exact same request re-enters this whole block from scratch on the
very next schedule() call -- roughly every 60-70ms under this
deployment's MTP spec-decode cadence, observed at 100+ times in under
ten seconds for one stuck request.

Investigated and ruled out caching the connector's answer directly:
`OffloadingConnectorScheduler.get_num_new_matched_tokens()` (offloading/
scheduler.py) has real side effects on every call -- it clears and
re-derives the request's per-group offload block-id tracking, resets
`req_status.num_locally_computed_tokens`, and calls `self._touch()`,
which protects the hit chunks from eviction. Skipping that call on a
"cached" tick would skip that eviction-protection touch too -- actively
counterproductive under exactly the memory-pressure conditions that
cause this stall -- and could leave `req_status`'s internal state stale
relative to whatever boundary is actually used if allocation succeeds
right after. So the connector call itself, and the block references
`get_computed_blocks[_for_connector]` return, are NEVER cached by this
patch -- every attempt that's actually made still redoes the full,
correct, freshly-verified dance, side effects included.

Fix instead: back off retrying only once a *complete* dance has already
run and STILL failed admission right after -- i.e., cut how often the
dance is attempted, not what it computes when attempted. The reconcile
branch stamps `request._hit_diverged_reconcile_ts = time.monotonic()`
the moment it runs (a plain attribute on Request -- not a dataclass
field, not slotted, so this is safe and self-cleans with the request's
own lifetime, no separate registry to leak or garbage-collect). At the
top of the waiting-queue walk, before any lookup work, a request whose
stamp is younger than VLLM_HIT_DIVERGED_RETRY_COOLDOWN_S (default 0.3s,
0 disables this entirely) is popped and deferred to step_skipped_waiting
instead -- the exact same "pop, defer, try the next waiting request"
pattern the lora/blocked-status/stale-output-token skips just below
already use in this identical loop, so a smaller request behind it can
still be scheduled this step, and admission order/fairness semantics are
unchanged from today's upstream behavior otherwise. If we see this
request again within the cooldown, it can only be because admission
failed right after the stamp was set (a successfully-admitted request
leaves the waiting queue and is never peeked here again), so nothing
about GPU or offload state could plausibly have changed in between --
and once the cooldown elapses, the request competes again exactly as
before, so a genuine change (blocks freed up, an in-flight store
completes) is never delayed by more than the cooldown.

Net effect: at the default 0.3s cooldown, a multi-second admission stall
drops from ~15 full dance-repeats/second to ~3/second for the affected
request -- most of the wasted connector I/O and local reconcile work
disappears, worst-case added admission latency is bounded at 300ms, and
HIT_DIVERGED reconcile safety semantics are completely unchanged (same
tokens discarded, same fallback taken, just attempted less often while
genuinely blocked on resources rather than data availability).

Verification: syntax-checked (ast.parse), `verify.sh --no-server` green.
Not yet observed against a live recurrence of the high-contention
scenario that exposed this.
